[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"HTTP Status Codes Deep Dive: Best Practices for REST APIs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"HTTP status codes are the backbone of RESTful API communication. They provide immediate context about the outcome of a client request. Mastering how and when to use 2xx, 3xx, 4xx, and 5xx status codes ensures your API is predictable, robust, and easy to consume."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Success Status Codes (2xx)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 2xx class of status codes indicates that the client's request was successfully received, understood, and accepted."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"200 OK: Standard response for successful HTTP requests. The actual response payload depends on the request method (e.g., GET returns the resource, PUT/PATCH returns the updated resource)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"201 Created: Essential for POST requests that successfully create a new resource. Always include a Location header pointing to the newly created resource's URI."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"204 No Content: Used when the request was successful, but there is no representation to return. Commonly used for DELETE requests or asynchronous background operations."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"HTTP/1.1 201 Created
Content-Type: application/json
Location: /api/v1/users/42

{
  "id": 42,
  "name": "Jane Doe"
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Redirection Status Codes (3xx)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Redirection codes inform the client that further action is needed to complete the request, typically navigating to a different URL."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"301 Moved Permanently: The resource has a new permanent URI. Future requests should use the new URL provided in the Location header."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"304 Not Modified: Used for conditional requests. If the client uses caching headers like If-None-Match, a 304 tells the client to use their locally cached copy, saving bandwidth."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Client Error Status Codes (4xx)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"4xx status codes indicate that the client seems to have erred, such as malformed request syntax, missing authentication, or requesting non-existent resources."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"400 Bad Request: A generic catch-all for client-side errors when no specific 4xx code applies (e.g., missing query parameters or invalid JSON payload)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"401 Unauthorized: The request lacks valid authentication credentials. The response must include a WWW-Authenticate header."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"403 Forbidden: The server understood the request, but refuses to authorize it. Unlike 401, authenticating will not help; the user lacks the necessary permissions."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"404 Not Found: The requested resource could not be found on the server."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"409 Conflict: Indicates a request conflict, such as trying to register an email address that already exists in the database."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"422 Unprocessable Entity: The request was well-formed (valid JSON syntax), but semantic validation failed (e.g., age must be a positive integer)."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "error": "Validation Failed",
  "fields": {
    "email": "The email format is invalid."
  }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Server Error Status Codes (5xx)"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"5xx codes indicate that the server encountered an unexpected condition that prevented it from fulfilling the request."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"500 Internal Server Error: A generic catch-all for unexpected server behavior, such as an unhandled exception in the application code."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"503 Service Unavailable: The server is currently unable to handle the request due to temporary overloading or maintenance of the server. Consider providing a Retry-After header."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Best Practices"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never return 200 OK with an error message in the JSON body. Always use proper 4xx or 5xx status codes."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be specific with client errors. Use 409 for conflicts and 422 for validation rules rather than defaulting everything to 400."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep error payloads consistent. Return a standardized error structure containing fields like error, message, and timestamp."}]
